

- Key factors that affect RL 
	1. Model (baseline vs SDF'd)
		- SDF can change the 
			- likelihood of exploring trajectories
			- inductive bias, i.e. how certain trajectories induce generalisation
	2. Trajectory
		- Several ways to shape exploration
			- Hardcode
				- Condition heavily and run, then swap with other conditioning
			- Warm-start SFT to make something salient
			- Different conditioning in system prompt
	3. Conditionalisation of the trajectory (IP)
		- Affects both exploration and which circuits active (both exploration and ~quasi inductive bias)
	4. Reward 
		- Which trajectories are positively/negatively/not reinforced

- Close related work
	- Recontextualisation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.19027)
		- Sample trajectories with one prompt, then train/gradient step with other prompt
		- Already works quite well, points to exploration having strong impact (1. model untouched, 3. normal conditionalisation)
		- Leetcode setting: one system prompt named the loophole and used identically at generation and training -> reward hacking from 79.1% to 0.2%. 
			- Pretty solid evidence for exploration controlling learning
	- Natural EM from RH (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18397)
		- SDF changes exploration (hacks after SDF)
		- IP changes both 2. trajectory and 3. conditionalisation; 3. clearly won and bad thing wasn't learned
	- Exploration hacking (https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/eeFFpKCDWE9gjfzsk/exploration-hacking-can-llms-learn-to-resist-rl-training-2)
		- SFT model to not explore -> model does not explore and does not learn
		- 1. model changed, but clearly the limiting factor is limiting 2. exploration
	- Reward laundering (https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/fPWP4rHPLqKKHKe6B/reward-laundering-llms-can-gain-unintended-behaviors-by)
		- 

- More conventional / theoretical related RL work
	- Entropy collapse (https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.22617)
		- Models become very sure about what to do and policy mode collapses
		- Little exploration beyond current policy
		- Mathematically, entropy bounds performance
	- Primacy bias in RL (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/nikishin22a.html)
		- RL relies more on early interactions
		- Policy / exploration very path dependent on first steps
		- Off policy worse than on policy
	- RL's razor (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04259)
		- SFT easily big distribution shift from pretraining prior
		- KL penalty keeps models close to the base model
		- Hence RL should explore close to base model and retain more capabilities/performance than SFT


My one sentence summary would be 

"Exploration shapes what can be learned, conditionalisation decides how far learning is generalised"


Core RQ seems like 

"Does SDF only influence exploration or also the inductive bias?" 

- We already sketched this, and goes nicely with Recontextualisation (Azarbal et al.)
- Have to still think about the details
	- How to isolate effect
	- How bad is it the trajectories begin to drift super off-policy
